{
  "notes": "The race map: inner-v7b's map7 (also inner-v7's, the same raster), 400 x 287 cells over the Northeast. coast and border are its raster as flat [x, y, x, y, ...] cell lists (border is drawn dotted, faintly); race.js resamples them to whatever grid fits the screen (never above 400 x 287). nodes and edges are its highways in the same cells; a season draws only the ones that lead to its venues (the shortest roads from home to each, and the highways between junctions on them), two cells across, and he walks only on those. Round 4 (2026-10-07, the owner: thicker highways, and New York and Flushing not crowded once they are): with the roads that thick the network is kept simple: a ring (Boston, Worcester, Springfield, Hartford, New Haven, the shore by New London, Rhode Island) and one trunk from New Haven down I-95 through the Bronx (BRX) and Midtown (JAV, the Javits Center: New York's stop, on the trunk) to Newark and Mercer, with Flushing on a spur of its own from the Bronx and Rockland Lake on a spur of its own off I-95 at Port Chester (PCH; I-287 over the Tappan Zee). Gone: the square of the Bronx, the George Washington Bridge, Midtown and Flushing, the I-84 diagonal by Danbury and the Tappan Zee junction, Providence as a junction beside Rhode Island's stop, and the two cross-links Hartford - Providence and Worcester - Providence (a walk between two venues follows the ring). land is the map's land as runs of cells per row ('x0-x1 x2-x3'; the sea flooded from the open Atlantic, the coast blocking it; Long Island, Block Island and the islands count as land). terrain is real geography in [lon, lat], placed through proj: a few marks on the ranges (kind peak: a small summit, ridge: a short ridgeline) and lakes (reservoirs and ponds); race.js draws them, with a faint stipple over the rest of the land, as a whisper on what the season's roads, stops, names and pictures leave free, with a wide clear corridor round the roads (v7b's trees are gone). proj places a [lon, lat] on the grid: x = (lon - lon0) * kx, y = (lat1 - lat) * ky (fitted to v7b's seven venues; kx / ky = cos 41.5 deg). places names the stops the races are drawn at (lowercase, as the map writes them); races puts each race at a stop. A race not listed goes to the nearest stop within 12 cells, else to a stop of its own. Narragansett Bay redrawn by hand (2026-10-11, the owner: the geography under Rhode Island's stop looked untidy: map7's raster had the bay's passages one cell wide, its lines touching and crossing, the upper bay sealed off as land, a corner on the road and the state line through its water): one bay opening south from the stop's foot (its head set about three cells east of the Providence River, two cells of paper from the road), Aquidneck Island alone in it (Conanicut and Prudence left out: at a cell a kilometre their passages are one or two cells), three cells of water in the Sakonnet, and the Massachusetts line moved east of the bay's shore (down x 295, then south-east to its old course at 305, 116). coast, border and land rows 99 to 138 are that corner's; nothing else moved.",
